1|0@Anarcho_Capitalism^1440601762#cugbc7f:Anenome5$:Uh, the thing that's exceptional about Mein Kampf *is its racism, the racism of its author* and the institutional racism of Naziism that led to horrific crimes. Are you serious? When people talk about the Magna Carta, what's exceptional is that it's the world's first break from power, the first establishment of civil rights, etc., etc. Tbh, until you mentioned it, I'd never heard anyone mention it in a racist context. I looked up the details and there's a lot of ugly racism around Jews. I don't support any of that. But again, I don't believe the ground-breaking portions of the Magna Carta should be completely ignored just because it was also racist--which is what you seem to be saying. The error you're making is looking back with today's ethical progress and judging the past from that basis. It is completely unexceptional that the Magna Carta for its day was racist. Everyone was racist back then. There's absolutely no reason to point to the MC as racist therefore. Again, and for the last time, what was exceptional about it is what made it different from all other political documents of its day, and you do the past a gross disservice to ignore the one and focus on the other. I'm completely comfortable saying the MC's racism was ugly and horrible, while at the same time saying the political changes it pioneered are groundbreaking developments that changed the course of human history in a positive way, leading directly to the modern world. It sounds like you're incapable of doing that, and that's a mental handicap on your part. Edit: Looking at your post history you've got a chip on your shoulder about race. You should abandon libertarianism then, because sentiment like yours won't like the outcome. When you say: &gt; i would rather have a anti-racist state than a 'free' society that allowed outright oppression of historically marginalized people of colour. You are likely to try to use state power to oppress racism, whereas liberty creates room for private racism and only punishes it economically. &gt; I am one of the few true ancaps who stands not only against aggression, but stands against oppression in all its forms, including racism and subjugation against people of color. End state power and all institutional forms of that oppression ends. There is no need to oppose any other kind of oppression except state power. The rest can be handled by social pressure. 
2|0@Anarcho_Capitalism^1439771585#cu5dpqw:Anenome5$:***Rudyard Kipling (1865-1936)*** --- At Runnymede, at Runnymede, What say the reeds at Runnymede? The lissom reeds that give and take, That bend so far, but never break, They keep the sleepy Thames awake With tales of John at Runnymede. . At Runnymede, at Runnymede, Oh, hear the reeds at Runnymede: 'You musn't sell, delay, deny, A freeman's right or liberty. It wakes the stubborn Englishry, We saw 'em roused at Runnymede! . When through our ranks the Barons came, With little thought of praise or blame, But resolute to play the game, They lumbered up to Runnymede; And there they launched in solid line The first attack on Right Divine, The curt uncompromising "Sign!' They settled John at Runnymede. . At Runnymede, at Runnymede, Your rights were won at Runnymede! No freeman shall be fined or bound, Or dispossessed of freehold ground, Except by lawful judgment found And passed upon him by his peers. Forget not, after all these years, The Charter signed at Runnymede.' . And still when mob or Monarch lays Too rude a hand on English ways, The whisper wakes, the shudder plays, Across the reeds at Runnymede. And Thames, that knows the moods of kings, And crowds and priests and suchlike things, Rolls deep and dreadful as he brings Their warning down from Runnymede! 
